Good 👍 that red line is a very common Power Apps issue—and it’s fixable.


---

❗ WHY YOU GET ERROR ON .Supplier.Id

).Supplier.Id   ❌

👉 Because:

Filter(...) returns a table

.Supplier.Id tries to access a column inside a nested record

Power Apps sometimes cannot resolve it directly



---

✅ FIX 1 (RECOMMENDED – USE ShowColumns)

Replace your formula with this:

Filter(
    'SE Supplier List',
    Section.Id = varSelectedSection.ID &&
    ID in
    ShowColumns(
        Filter(
            colAssignment,
            Section.Value = varSelectedSection.Title &&
            User.Email = User().Email
        ),
        "Supplier"
    ).Supplier.Id
)


---

❗ BUT if STILL ERROR → use the safest method 👇


---

✅ FIX 2 (BEST & CLEAN – USE COLLECTION)


---

🧩 STEP 1: Create filtered supplier IDs

In Dropdown_Section.OnChange or OnVisible:

ClearCollect(
    colAssignedSuppliers,
    ForAll(
        Filter(
            colAssignment,
            Section.Value = varSelectedSection.Title &&
            User.Email = User().Email
        ),
        Supplier.Id
    )
)


---

🧩 STEP 2: Use it in your gallery

Filter(
    'SE Supplier List',
    Section.Id = varSelectedSection.ID &&
    ID in colAssignedSuppliers
)


---

🧠 WHY THIS WORKS

colAssignedSuppliers = [1, 2, 3]

Then:
Supplier.ID in [1,2,3] ✅


---

🧪 DEBUG TIP

Add label:

Text = Concat(colAssignedSuppliers, Value & ", ")

👉 Should show IDs like:

1, 2, 3


---

🚨 COMMON ERRORS


---

❌ Supplier.Id not recognized

👉 Check:

Supplier column is Lookup

Not text column



---

❌ User.Email error

👉 Use fallback:

User.Claims = "i:0#.f|membership|" & User().Email


---

❌ Empty result

👉 Means:

No assignment for that user + section


